WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.540
Let u be odd and greater than one. Use the
prime-indexed subset roots from Node four,

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.980
and write c sub S, d sub S, and
g sub S for their evaluations.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:15.300
The preceding steps prove
R equals W equals one.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.100
Unpack R as the alternating
product of d sub S over c sub S,

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.360
and W as the alternating
product of g sub S over c sub S.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.440
Even subsets contribute
positive first powers,

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.380
and odd subsets contribute
negative first powers.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.000
Every denominator is a unit
in our evaluation ring.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.380
Moving the inverse factors across gives an

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.720
exact equality between
the even and odd products.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.200
For u equal to three, there are
only two inputs, one and omega.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.180
The d over c ratio at one
equals that ratio at omega,

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.840
and the same is true for g over c.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.720
Define lambda as c of
omega divided by c of one.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.960
Both values have magnitude three,
so lambda has magnitude one.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:06.600
Rearrange the two ratio equalities:
d of omega is lambda times d of one,

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.640
and g of omega is lambda times g of one.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.520
The same multiplier also
carries c of one to c of omega.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.540
This forward multiplier is
the inverse of Delta of c.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.600
The three values share a rotation.
They need not be the same point:

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.020
the rotation preserves
their relative positions.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.360
Now recall the exact integral
combination E of P: d of P minus c of P,

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:36.420
divided by two, plus g of P minus
c of P, divided by one plus i.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.940
These polynomial quotients
were formed before reduction.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.400
Substitute the three
shared-rotation identities.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.140
Factor lambda from both differences.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.840
The result is E of omega
equals lambda times E of one.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.100
Divide this last identity by c of omega,
which equals lambda times c of one.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.360
The common multiplier cancels,

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.240
leaving E of omega over c of omega
equal to E of one over c of one.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.880
We divide by the nonzero unit
values of c, never by E of one.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.360
Thus the argument also covers
E of one equal to zero.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.360
The E arrow need not lie
on the radius-three circle.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.240
Here is a numerical schematic,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:24.380
not a construction satisfying all Hadamard
conditions. Take c of one equal to three,

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.980
d of one equal to negative three,
and g of one equal to three i.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.160
Rotate all three by lambda equal to omega.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.340
The combination gives E of one equal
to negative three plus three i.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.420
After dividing by the
corresponding c value,

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.560
both normalized E values
are negative one plus i.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.240
The two normalized arrows coincide.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.980
The raw E values need not be equal.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.000
They differ by lambda, which may
also have a nontrivial residue.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.980
Equality after division by c does not
assert equality of their raw residues.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:02.600
We have assigned no all-ones
residue to the schematic values.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:08.900
For several distinct prime divisors of u,
retain the full subset-product identities.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.520
We cannot infer one common rotation
between every pair of inputs.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.120
Keep two outputs separate: the normalized
equality for the two-input case,

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.260
and the general exact products.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.720
Node seven uses the normalized
equality for the guiding example,

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.660
and product expansion
for the general argument.
